Aerie-Purpose: Switch Chromium's Private AI client off, so the browser never builds PrivateAiService and never fetches a TLS certificate or opens a TLS websocket to Google's Private AI servers.
Aerie-Upstream-Area: components/private_ai/features.cc (kPrivateAi)
Aerie-Kind: hook

Chromium 158 added components/private_ai (network annotations private_ai_cert_fetch and
private_ai_tls_client). The feature kPrivateAi is on by default, and
PrivateAiServiceFactory builds the service when PrivateAiService::CanPrivateAiBeEnabled() is
true, which asks kPrivateAi first. With the feature off the factory returns no service and no
client is created. The Bench zero_traffic check saw no request on the 158 build, but that was
luck, not a switch: a bump that sets a key, or a page that starts it, would phone home. The
source gate private_ai_off checks the default.

--- a/components/private_ai/features.cc
+++ b/components/private_ai/features.cc
@@ -11,7 +11,7 @@
 
 namespace private_ai {
 
-BASE_FEATURE(kPrivateAi, base::FEATURE_ENABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
+BASE_FEATURE(kPrivateAi, base::FEATURE_DISABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
 
 BASE_FEATURE(kPrivateAiServerAttestation, base::FEATURE_ENABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
 
